WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.772
An equation alone does not determine a process: the same heat equation describes a rod in a fridge and in an oven.

2
00:00:09.772 --> 00:00:12.772
The conditions make the difference.

3
00:00:12.772 --> 00:00:19.972
In this part we study initial and boundary conditions, when a problem is well-posed,

4
00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:25.286
and the Dirichlet and Neumann problems for Laplace’s equation.

5
00:00:25.536 --> 00:00:29.805
Initial conditions give the state at time zero.

6
00:00:29.805 --> 00:00:38.434
The heat equation has a first-order time derivative, so one condition: the initial temperature.

7
00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:46.881
The wave equation has a second-order one, so two: the initial shape and the initial velocity.

8
00:00:46.881 --> 00:00:52.694
Laplace’s equation has no time at all, only boundary conditions.

9
00:00:52.944 --> 00:00:58.168
Boundary conditions are given at the ends of the rod for all times.

10
00:00:58.168 --> 00:01:02.768
First kind, Dirichlet: the temperature of the end is given.

11
00:01:02.768 --> 00:01:10.565
Second kind, Neumann: the flux through the end is given; an insulated end has u sub x equal to zero.

12
00:01:10.565 --> 00:01:18.830
Third kind, Robin: a combination of u sub x and u, used when the end exchanges heat with the surroundings.

13
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:25.656
Example: consider the steady state of a homogeneous rod with no internal heat source.

14
00:01:25.656 --> 00:01:33.161
In this model the time derivative is zero and the equation reduces to u double prime equals zero.

15
00:01:33.161 --> 00:01:36.798
Therefore u equals A plus B x, a straight line.

16
00:01:36.798 --> 00:01:41.750
The left end is at one hundred degrees, so A equals one hundred.

17
00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:48.326
At the right end we have a Robin condition: u prime at one plus u at one equals zero.

18
00:01:48.576 --> 00:01:57.072
Substitute into the Robin condition: B plus one hundred plus B equals zero, so B equals minus fifty.

19
00:01:57.072 --> 00:02:00.725
Answer: u equals one hundred minus fifty x.

20
00:02:00.725 --> 00:02:06.163
The right end cools to fifty degrees because it gives heat away.

21
00:02:06.163 --> 00:02:12.110
Check: u prime is minus fifty, u at one is fifty, and the sum is zero.

22
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:26.465
The graph shows two profiles for this particular homogeneous steady source-free model: a line from one hundred to fifty with a Robin condition and a line with Dirichlet data at both ends.

23
00:02:26.465 --> 00:02:31.142
The profile is linear here because u double prime equals zero.

24
00:02:31.142 --> 00:02:37.629
With an internal heat source, spatially varying conductivity, or other physical terms,

25
00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:41.174
a steady profile need not be linear in general.

26
00:02:41.424 --> 00:02:49.458
By Hadamard, a problem is well-posed if a solution exists, is unique, and depends continuously on the data.

27
00:02:49.458 --> 00:02:57.418
Under standard regularity assumptions, the Dirichlet problem for Laplace’s equation has a unique solution.

28
00:02:57.418 --> 00:03:01.923
For the Neumann problem the total boundary flux must vanish,

29
00:03:01.923 --> 00:03:06.653
and the solution is determined only up to an additive constant.

30
00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:11.534
The Cauchy problem for Laplace’s equation is generally ill-posed.

31
00:03:11.784 --> 00:03:15.902
Question: the end of a rod is insulated.

32
00:03:15.902 --> 00:03:18.682
Which condition is correct?

33
00:03:18.682 --> 00:03:23.006
Choose an answer and read the explanation.
